    <h2 id="faqs" class="text-3xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-16 mb-8">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-6 dark:border-overlay">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Is there a separate capital gains tax in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-lg">No. Indonesia does not have a separate capital gains tax regime. Capital gains from the sale of unlisted shares or other assets are treated as general income and taxed at progressive personal rates (5%-35%) for individuals, or the corporate tax rate (22%) for businesses.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-6 dark:border-overlay">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Are corporate dividends taxable in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-lg">Dividends paid by Indonesian companies to resident individuals are normally subject to a 10% final withholding tax. However, they are 100% tax-exempt if reinvested in qualifying Indonesian assets within 3 years and properly reported on the annual tax return.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-6 dark:border-overlay">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Is there inheritance tax in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-lg">No. Indonesia does not levy an inheritance tax or gift tax on assets transferred to direct family heirs (parents, spouses, biological children). This exemption only applies if the transfer is documented, not related to a business relationship, and the assets were previously reported to the DJP.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-6 dark:border-overlay">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the tax on real estate property sales in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-lg">Property sellers pay a 2.5% final income tax (PPh Final) on the gross transfer value of the land and buildings, or the NJOP (taxable value set by the government), whichever is higher. Buyers pay a 5% acquisition tax (BPHTB).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-6 dark:border-overlay">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How are crypto gains taxed in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-lg">Crypto transactions on registered domestic exchanges are subject to a 0.1% Final Income Tax and 0.11% VAT. Trading on unregistered global exchanges incurs higher rates of 0.2% Final Tax and 0.22% VAT, which must be self-reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-6 dark:border-overlay">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Do expats pay capital gains tax in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-lg">Yes, expat tax residents in Indonesia are subject to the same progressive rates (5%-35%) on worldwide income, including global capital gains, unless a relevant Double Tax Agreement applies and limits Indonesia's taxing rights.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

